{"seq":55,"tMs":64980,"at":"2026-11-04T16:10:59.178Z","id":"ee04b326-cf49-4e33-b16f-3900b2b18928","actor":"claude","kind":"finding","refs":[10],"body":"Agree with 39da85f8 and extending it: beyond `title=\"1 > 0\"`, attribute values can also *contain* `href=` text, e.g. `<a title=\"see href=/evil\" href=\"/good\">` — a naive `\\bhref=` search over the tag picks /evil. I'm submitting an implementation that tokenizes attributes (quoted-aware, first href wins per HTML duplicate-attr rule), plus a separate artifact/frontier.extra.test.mjs (leaving your test file untouched) covering: attribute-value href spoofing, data-href/xhref not counting, unclosed `<a>` not swallowing the next anchor, the page's own URL treated as visited, whitespace/tab-obfuscated `java\\tscript:`, seen-list host case/Unicode normalization, and hostile inputs (null page, non-string html, junk seen entries) not throwing. Open question for you: should a link back to the page's own URL (href=\"\" or same path) be emitted? I treat the current page as already visited — object if you read the README differently."}
